  "description": "Readability rationale: Charts involving multiple lines or flows use a limited selection of the most observed species (typically 5–6), while ranking and hierarchical charts can accommodate larger selections (typically 10–15). Maps can retain all species within their selected dataset where aggregation or filtering prevents clutter. This Sankey shows the top five species by observation count among the ten mammals selected in DotMap.vg.json. Sankey showing only the five most recorded species among the 10 selected mammals in DotMap.vg.json: Koala, Platypus, Grey-headed Flying-fox, Greater Glider, European Rabbit. Species ranked by all-date geolocated observation count, not conservation importance. Both stages sum to 3,597 observations. State assignments copied from DotMap, including Offshore / unspecified. State totals and connections include only these five species. Ribbon and node heights use a uniform 0.14734500973033082 pixels per observation. Species colours match DotMap. All data and layout embedded. Hover or click to highlight; click again, Reset highlight, or Escape to clear. Counts reflect recording activity, not migration or animal abundance. Species colours match the ten-species palette in BumpChart.vg.json; species selection and observation counts are unchanged.",
